    "reading": {
        "body": "North Carolina's tracked data center capacity totals 40 MW across 19 facilities, with a single campus, CyrusOne's Durham complex, accounting for all 40 MW of metered power in the dataset.\n\nThat concentration matters because it means the state's measurable power footprint is not distributed across its clusters but effectively recorded at one address. The remaining 18 facilities appear in the data without megawatt figures, so the true statewide total is unknown and likely higher. Charlotte, Durham, and Raleigh hold most of the named facilities, with Meta operating four adjacently located buildings in Rutherford County that the data lists without power figures.\n\nThe data covers geography and operator identity reasonably well but is thin on capacity. Square footage is given for only two facilities, CyrusOne Durham at 420,000 square feet and TierPoint Charlotte at 20,000 square feet, a gap that prevents any useful inference about buildout scale from floor area. For readers tracking how physical infrastructure constrains AI compute, North Carolina appears as a real but incompletely measured market: the buildings are identified, the power behind most of them is not.",
